> > * A game that's selectively unbalanced can be a lot more fun than a
> >   balanced one!
> 
> If balancing is removed *selectively*, I'd say that's pretty balanced.

But I'm referring to balancing in a different way there.

It is easy to create a perfectly balanced game.  Rock, Scissors, Paper
is perfectly balanced.  I'd say it's too balanced, in fact -- the
different options each have one strength and one weakness.  There's no
reason to pick one over another.    It can be made more interesting by
looking at an iterated version of the game, while two people play
successively and try to guess what the other will throw, but without
that angle, it's not very interesting.

It is more difficult to upset the obvious balance in a way that makes
it more interesting, while not destroying the game.  That's what
Nethack does, successfully, in many places.


