> No, but those games often have potions of invulnerability, which basically
> allow you to do the same. Not by instant killing ability, but by
> not-being-able-to-be-killed-yourself ability, giving you enough time to
> achieve the same.

Which game, in particular, are you speaking of?

In any case, even if there is a potion of invulnerability, consumable
items in these games do not typically have the risk associated with
cockatrice corpses, and tend to either be so rare as to be almost
useless, or so common (purchasable in a shop somewhere) that the
management of their numbers adds little to the game.

This is true, in part, because there's a fundamental design difference
between console (and, increasingly, computer) RPGs and Roguelikes: the
former are designed so that almost all situations are escapable,
starting with that assumption and designing out from there, whereas
Roguelikes create a random world and try to give the player enough
tools so that they can devise a way of survival on his own.

